{{also|Sais|šais}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Via Hindustani, from Arabic {{l|ar|سائس}}, from {{Arab|[[سوس]]}} ‘to tend a horse’.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/saɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{context|India|lang=en}} A [[groom]], or servant with responsibility for the horses.
#* '''1888''', Here all trace of him was lost, until a '''''sais''''' or groom met me on the Simla Mall with this extraordinary note — Rudyard Kipling, ‘Miss Youghal's ''Sais''’, ''Plain Tales from the Hills'' (Folio Society 2007, p. 25)

